/** Fully reordered chapter retellings where scene weaving left events out of sequence. */
export const manualTeenRetellings: Record<string, { b1: string; b2More: [string,string,string]; sharedMore: string; sharedIndex?: number }> = {
  'book6-treasure-island-1': {
    sharedMore:'Jim holds the candle while his mother looks through the chest. She counts only the coins owed to the inn, although he can hear Pew’s cane tapping outside. Beneath the clothes Jim finds an oilskin packet. He takes it as the pirates reach the door, and the two slip into the dark. From their hiding place Jim hears the men overturn furniture inside the inn.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`Jim Hawkins lives with his parents at the Admiral Benbow inn beside the sea. An old sailor called Billy Bones arrives carrying a locked chest. He pays Jim to watch for a one-legged seaman and spends many hours looking toward the road and the water. At night he drinks, sings frightening songs, and tells stories that make the guests leave. Dr. Livesey visits the inn and warns Billy that his drinking will harm him. When Black Dog, another sailor, arrives, the men argue and draw their swords. Billy collapses after the fight, and the doctor treats him.

Jim's father dies, and Jim and his mother must manage the inn while Billy grows weaker. A blind pirate named Pew comes with a black spot, a warning that Billy's old shipmates want his papers. Billy dies soon afterward. Jim and his mother know the pirates may return. They open his chest to collect the money he owes them while the road outside grows noisy.

The pirates search the chest, but the packet is gone. Riders arrive, and the pirates scatter; Pew falls beneath a horse in the confusion. Jim takes the packet to Livesey and Squire Trelawney. They open it and find Flint's map showing the island where the old pirate buried his treasure. Trelawney begins planning a voyage to the island. Jim has escaped the men who wanted the map, yet the papers now place him at the start of another dangerous journey.`,
    b2More:[
      'Billy often asks whether Jim has seen the one-legged man, but he says little about why he fears him. His locked chest and sudden anger make the inn feel less safe to the boy who works there.',
      'Pew presses the black spot into Billy’s hand and leaves him with a deadline. Billy’s sudden death gives Jim no chance to ask what the mark means. His mother insists on an honest account of the money even when she hears footsteps outside.',
      'The map has marks that the doctor and squire study closely. Trelawney can supply a ship and crew, while Livesey knows that the men from the inn may still be hunting for the same packet. Jim listens as their plan takes shape.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-treasure-island-2': {
    sharedMore:'At Bristol Jim sees the new ship in the harbour and visits Silver at the Spy-glass tavern. Black Dog is there but runs away when Jim recognizes him. Silver sends men after the visitor and acts surprised that a pirate would enter his place. Jim reports what he saw, while Silver continues to speak warmly to the passengers.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Squire Trelawney buys the Hispaniola and hires a crew for the voyage. Jim travels to Bristol and meets the ship's cook, Long John Silver. Silver has one leg, like the man Billy Bones feared, but his friendly manner wins Jim's trust. Captain Smollett is less comfortable with the preparations. He learns that sailors know where the ship is going even though the purpose was meant to be secret. He asks Trelawney to move the weapons and powder close to the loyal passengers. The squire dislikes the warning, but Dr. Livesey supports the captain.

The Hispaniola sails toward the island. Silver works hard in the galley and speaks kindly to Jim. The boy watches him organize the crew and begins to forget his first worry about the missing leg. Smollett remains careful while the ship crosses the sea. One evening Jim climbs into an apple barrel to take the last fruit. Before he can leave, Silver and other sailors gather nearby. Jim stays hidden and listens.

Silver tells the men that he once served under Captain Flint. He has persuaded many of the crew to join him, but he wants them to wait until the honest passengers have found the treasure. Then the pirates will seize it. Jim hears how Silver calms a sailor who wants to act sooner. After the men move away, Jim leaves the barrel and tells Livesey, Trelawney, and Smollett what he heard. They can no longer trust most of their crew. The island is close, and they must decide whom they can rely on before anyone knows that Jim heard the plan.`,
    b2More:[
      'Smollett notices that the sailors have been given too much information. He wants the trusted party to sleep and keep its arms together, rather than leave weapons where mutineers could take them. The others agree before the ship leaves port.',
      'Silver has a public face that makes suspicion difficult. He jokes with Jim and treats ordinary work as if nothing is wrong. The long crossing gives him time to hold his followers together.',
      'Jim hears the men discuss when to strike and realizes that the cook has been patient for a reason. He cannot move without shaking the barrel. Later the loyal group speaks quietly because warning the pirates too soon would leave them outnumbered.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-treasure-island-4': {
    sharedMore:'Jim uses Ben Gunn’s small boat at night, fighting the current as he reaches the Hispaniola. He cuts the anchor rope and lets the tide carry the ship away from the pirates’ landing place. When he climbs aboard later, he finds one sailor dead and Hands injured. Hands helps steer for a while, then reaches for a weapon.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Jim leaves the stockade and finds Ben Gunn's little boat. At night he paddles toward the Hispaniola and cuts its anchor rope. The current pulls the ship away from the pirates. After a dangerous trip in the small craft, Jim climbs aboard the drifting vessel. Israel Hands is wounded but promises to help him steer. Jim guides the ship toward a quiet inlet, watching Hands carefully. When Hands attacks, Jim climbs into the rigging and fires at him. Hands falls into the water. Jim has saved the ship, but the loyal party does not yet know where he is.

He returns to the stockade in the dark and discovers that Silver's men now occupy it. They capture Jim, and some want to kill him. Silver stops them, hoping to use the boy in a bargain with Livesey. The pirates challenge Silver's leadership and give him a black spot, but he shows them Flint's map and persuades them to follow him. The doctor visits Jim and asks him to escape. Jim refuses because he has promised Silver not to run. Livesey leaves with that promise still in place.

Silver leads the pirates and Jim to the place marked on the map. They find an empty pit. The gold has already been moved by Ben Gunn. The angry men turn on Silver, but Livesey and the loyal group arrive and drive them off. Jim learns how Ben hid the treasure after his years alone on the island. The survivors carry it to the ship and sail away. Silver slips away at a port on the return voyage. Jim reaches home with a share of the gold, though he does not wish to go back to that island.`,
    b2More:[
      'The small boat is hard to control in the dark, and the tide carries Jim farther than he planned. On board he keeps his distance from Hands, whose wound has not made him safe. The duel ends only when Jim can reach the mast.',
      'Silver must defend his leadership while guarding a captive whose value the others do not see. Jim has to trust the promise of a pirate who has betrayed him before. The doctor knows more than he tells them about the map.',
      'The empty pit shocks the men who thought they were close to a fortune. Ben Gunn’s secret explains why the doctor could give Silver the map. The loyal group loads the treasure while the surviving pirates are left on the island.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-time-machine-1': {
    sharedMore:'The traveller puts the control levers in his pocket before walking away from the machine. From a hill he can see the strange white figure and the great hall where the Eloi gather. They serve him fruit, then drift away from his questions. He returns to the machine before night, expecting to find it where he left it.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`The Time Traveller invites guests to dinner and asks them to imagine time as another direction through which a person might move. They doubt him. He sets a small model on a table and asks a guest to touch its lever. The model vanishes. Some guests search beneath the table while others suspect a trick. The inventor tells them that a larger machine waits in his workshop. When they gather again a week later, he is late. He appears with torn clothes and dirt on his face, eats, and begins to describe the journey he has just made.

He sat on the large machine and pressed its controls. Day and night flashed past as years rushed around him. Buildings changed and vanished. He stopped in the distant year 802,701 and fell beside the machine. The world around him was warm and green. Small people called the Eloi came to meet him. They offered fruit and led him into a great building. He tried to learn their words and ask how they lived, but they quickly tired of his questions. Their easy life made him think at first that future people faced no work or danger.

The traveller returned to the place where he had left his machine. It was gone. Marks in the grass showed that something heavy had been moved, though he had kept the control levers with him. Nearby stood a white stone figure with closed bronze doors beneath it. He searched the ground and tried to question the Eloi, but they gave him no clear answer. Without the machine he could not return to his own time. He had arrived ready to explore; now he had to find what someone had taken.`,
    b2More:[
      'The guests watch the space where the model stood, but no ordinary hiding place explains its disappearance. The Time Traveller does not force them to believe him. He says the larger device will make the question harder to dismiss.',
      'As the years race past, the sun seems to rise and set faster and faster. The traveller stops the machine suddenly and looks around at unfamiliar plants and buildings. He keeps the levers in his pocket before walking toward the people.',
      'The stone figure is large enough to hide an entrance. The traveller sees no way through its bronze doors. He cannot yet tell whether the Eloi moved the machine or whether another group shares their world.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-time-machine-2': {
    sharedMore:'The traveller spends daylight circling the stone figure and studying the grass. He points to the bronze doors, hoping the Eloi will explain them, but they move away or laugh. Weena stays near him after he has helped her. Her fear when evening comes makes his search for the missing machine more urgent.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`The Time Traveller lives among the Eloi while he searches for his missing machine. They offer him food and shelter, but they cannot explain where the device has gone. One day he sees a young Eloi carried away by water while others keep playing. He jumps in and brings her to shore. Her name is Weena. She follows him afterward and gives him flowers. The friendship helps him learn more about the people around him, although their language remains hard for him to understand.

Near the white stone figure, the traveller finds marks in the grass where the machine stood. He knows it cannot have travelled away through time because he kept its levers. The bronze doors below the figure are shut. He feels around their edges and asks the Eloi about them, but they avoid the question. Weena becomes frightened as the light fades. Other Eloi gather together rather than stay alone. Their beautiful halls feel less safe at night than they did by day.

The traveller notices a pale figure moving among the bushes. It disappears down a well. He goes closer and feels air coming from below; he can hear distant machinery too. The Eloi live above ground, enjoying fruit and sunlight, but another kind of person may be working underneath them. He thinks again of the closed doors beneath the stone figure. As darkness grows, Weena stays beside him. He has found signs of people who might know where his machine is, but he does not yet know whether they will help him.`,
    b2More:[
      'The Eloi who watched Weena fall into the water did not try to rescue her. The traveller is surprised by their indifference. Weena’s gratitude gives him a companion in a world where he understands almost no one.',
      'Narrow tracks lead across the grass toward the stone structure. The traveller tests the doors but cannot shift them. His own missing levers remain in his pocket, proof that the machine itself is still somewhere nearby.',
      'At the well the air moves steadily upward, as if machines are working below. A pale face appears for a moment and vanishes. The traveller begins to understand why the Eloi avoid dark places and why their answers have been so thin.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-time-machine-3': {
    sharedMore:'He enters the Palace with Weena and looks through dusty rooms that once held exhibits. A metal bar may serve as a weapon, while the matches and camphor will help him make light. They cannot stay there forever. The long walk back toward the stone figure will take them across open land and a wood.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`The traveller climbs down a well after seeing a pale figure vanish into it. Deep below the ground he finds the Morlocks. Their large eyes suit the darkness, and machines run around their workrooms. He first thinks they may serve the Eloi above, but their hands reach toward him and he has to escape. Back in the sunlight, he tells Weena little of what he saw. Her fear of the dark now makes sense. He is certain that the people below can come up at night, and he still has no machine with which to leave.

The traveller and Weena set out for the Palace of Green Porcelain, a distant building above the valley. It was once a museum, but its displays are broken and dust covers the rooms. He searches for anything that might protect them. He finds a metal bar, matches, and camphor. Fire makes the Morlocks retreat, so the matches are especially valuable. Weena walks close to him as they leave the Palace. Their journey back is long, and daylight is fading before they reach the place where the machine disappeared.

They enter a wood at dusk. The trees hide the last light, and Weena is tired from walking. The traveller gathers sticks and starts a fire. He puts the metal bar within reach and checks how many matches remain. Pale shapes move beyond the flames. He wants to lead Weena out of the wood before the fire dies, but the Morlocks are already near. For the moment the light keeps them back, and the two travellers wait together beside it.`,
    b2More:[
      'The narrow descent leaves little room to turn around. Below, the traveller sees pale workers among equipment that still functions. He has to find the well again while their hands brush against him.',
      'The Palace offers brief shelter but not a way home. He looks through cabinets and broken displays for things he can carry. Weena watches him gather what they will need after sunset.',
      'A fire can frighten the Morlocks, but it also needs fuel. The traveller searches the ground for dry wood while keeping Weena close. Sounds beyond the circle of light show that the danger has not passed.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-time-machine-4': {
    sharedMore:'The travellers have crossed the wood together, so Weena’s disappearance leaves the Time Traveller searching through smoke for the person who trusted him. He cannot find her. The rising heat drives the Morlocks away, but it gives him no answer about where she went. He goes on toward the white stone figure alone.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`A fire spreads through the wood while the traveller tries to keep the Morlocks away from Weena. Smoke covers the trees, and he loses her in the confusion. When morning comes, he searches among the burned branches but cannot find her. The Morlocks have fled the flames. Grieving and alone, he returns toward the white stone figure where his machine disappeared. The bronze doors beneath it now stand open. Inside he sees the machine, but the doors close behind him and the Morlocks rush in.

He reaches the machine and fits its control levers as the pale figures close around him. The floor seems to vanish when he starts it. He travels farther into the future and stops beside a cold sea. A huge crab-like creature moves on the shore under a dim red sun. He goes still farther and sees a world with almost no life. The lonely sight sends him back toward the time he knows. He reverses the machine and returns to his own workshop.

At dinner the Time Traveller tells his guests what happened. Some question his account, while the narrator listens and looks at the strange flowers Weena gave him. The traveller has brought those flowers and his damaged clothes back from the future, but they cannot show the others every part of the journey. The next day the narrator visits again. He sees the Time Traveller leave with the machine for another trip. He waits for him to return, yet the inventor does not come back.`,
    b2More:[
      'The traveller still has Weena’s flowers. He looks for her in the clearing despite the heat and falling ash. When he cannot find her, he has to continue without knowing exactly what happened in the flames.',
      'The Morlocks have placed the machine where he can see it, hoping to trap him in the closed chamber. He works the controls by touch as they reach for him. Then the room disappears into the racing years.',
      'The narrator returns to the house expecting another conversation. Instead he watches the inventor hurry away with his machine. Days pass with no news, leaving the narrator to think about both the journey he heard and the friend who has vanished.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-frankenstein-2': {
    sharedMore:'At home Elizabeth speaks of William’s last days and of Justine, who had helped care for him. Victor listens while the family tries to understand the murder. He remembers the figure he saw during the storm but says nothing. Soon the picture from William’s pocket is found among Justine’s belongings, and a new fear enters the house.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Victor recovers from the illness that followed his experiment. Henry Clerval cares for him, and the creature he brought to life is gone from the room where he left it. Victor avoids speaking about it. A letter from home tells him that his youngest brother William has been murdered near Geneva. He travels back, shocked by the loss. During a storm near the place where the boy died, he sees the creature for a moment in a flash of lightning. Victor fears that the being he abandoned may be responsible, but he has no proof that anyone else could examine.

The family servant Justine is accused because a small picture belonging to William is found among her things. She had cared for the child, and Elizabeth speaks for her. Victor knows the picture alone does not explain what happened. He wants to defend Justine, but he cannot tell the court about his hidden experiment without also explaining why he ran away from its result. The judges hear witnesses and the evidence they can see. Justine is frightened and cannot make them believe her innocence.

The court convicts Justine, and she is executed. Victor watches his family mourn her as well as William. He thinks again of the figure he saw near the murder site and of the body he made at university. He still cannot say what he suspects with certainty. Elizabeth has lost someone she trusted, and Victor has kept from her the one fact that might have changed the case. He leaves the household carrying a secret that has now reached beyond his laboratory.`,
    b2More:[
      'Victor had hoped that returning home would let him escape the memory of his work. William’s death takes him back to it at once. The storm briefly lights the creature’s outline and then hides it again.',
      'Elizabeth remembers how gently Justine treated the family. The picture in her possession makes the case look simple to others, but Victor knows a different explanation may exist. His silence leaves Elizabeth to argue without that knowledge.',
      'After the sentence, the house is quiet with two losses. Victor cannot undo the trial. He walks alone and thinks of the abandoned being whose movements he has never tried to follow.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-frankenstein-3': {
    sharedMore:'The creature watched a family from a hidden shelter. It learned words as the people spoke to one another and saw how they shared food, work, and affection. When it finally entered their home, the blind father listened kindly, but the others returned and drove it away. That rejection shaped the request it now brings to Victor.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`Victor travels into the mountains after the deaths in his family. On a glacier the creature he made approaches him. Victor wants to attack, but the creature asks him to listen first. It leads Victor to a shelter and describes waking alone, hungry, and cold. People fled when they saw its face. It hid near a cottage and learned language by watching the family who lived there. It helped them secretly by bringing wood. When it finally tried to speak with the blind father, the other family members returned and drove it away.

The creature tells Victor that loneliness has made its life unbearable. It asks him to create a companion like itself. If Victor agrees, the two beings will leave people and live far away. Victor fears what might happen if he refuses, but he also fears making another creature. He begins the work in a lonely place, keeping it secret from his family. As the second body takes shape, he imagines that it might reject the first or that they might harm others together.

The first creature comes to watch him. Victor tears apart the unfinished companion before he can bring it to life. The creature sees what he has done and vows to be with him on his wedding night. Victor clears away the pieces and takes them onto the water, but the threat follows him. He has refused the request; now he must return to people he loves while knowing that the being he made has promised revenge.`,
    b2More:[
      'The creature recalls the first fires it found and the food it learned to seek. Every attempt to approach people ended with fear or blows. It wants Victor to hear these events before judging it.',
      'In the cottage, it had watched the family teach a visitor new words. By listening through a crack in the wall, it learned to tell Victor what happened. Its request for company comes from those months of watching other people live together.',
      'Victor’s hands stop over the unfinished body when he sees the creature outside. Destroying it ends the work but not the danger. The visitor’s promise sends him back across the water with a fear he cannot share openly.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-frankenstein-4': {
    sharedMore:'Victor believes the wedding-night threat is aimed at him. He watches doors and paths for an attack while Elizabeth waits for him. A cry from her room shows that he misunderstood. After losing her, he tells an official of the creature and begs for help, but the chase soon takes him beyond the places where others can follow.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Victor returns to his family while the creature's threat hangs over him. His friend Henry Clerval is killed, and Victor is accused when the body is found. He is eventually cleared, but he goes home exhausted. He marries Elizabeth, hoping to protect her on the night the creature promised to come. Victor watches for an attack on himself. Then he hears Elizabeth cry out and finds her dead. His father dies soon afterward. Victor has now lost nearly everyone close to him.

He follows the creature across towns, mountains, and frozen land. The creature leaves signs that keep Victor on its trail, sometimes giving him just enough food to continue. Victor asks others for help, but the chase reaches places where they cannot follow for long. His strength begins to fail on the ice. Robert Walton's ship finds him and brings him aboard. Walton listens as Victor finishes the account he began after his rescue. Victor urges him to think about his crew's safety instead of risking their lives for his own ambition.

The sailors want to leave the ice and return home. Walton agrees. Victor dies before the ship can escape. Later Walton sees the creature beside Victor's body. It mourns the man it hated and the suffering it has caused. It says it will go away and end its own life, then disappears over the ice. Walton watches it leave. He cannot bring Victor back or follow the creature; he can only turn his ship toward home with the story he has heard.`,
    b2More:[
      'After Henry’s death, Victor has to answer questions about a crime he did not commit. He survives the accusation, but his secret remains with him when he returns to Elizabeth. He keeps watch at the wrong door.',
      'The creature’s trail leads through cold places where Victor can barely find food or rest. Walton’s sailors first see another large figure on the ice, then find Victor weakened on a sled. The explorer’s ship becomes the last place Victor tells his story.',
      'The creature speaks to Walton without asking for forgiveness. It says that revenge has left it alone with the deaths it caused. Walton hears the words, then sees the figure move away across the frozen sea.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-call-of-wild-1': {
    sharedMore:'On Judge Miller’s ranch Buck walked among the family and slept in a warm place. The gardener’s helper takes him away when men are paying for large dogs in the North. Buck struggles against the rope and the crate, but each train and ship carries him farther from home. No familiar person comes to bring him back.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Buck is a strong dog living with Judge Miller's family in California. He knows the paths around their ranch and expects food, warmth, and gentle voices. During the northern gold rush, a gardener's helper secretly sells him. Buck is tied, put into a crate, and passed from one stranger to another. He fights against the men who hold him, but he cannot return to the ranch. At one stop, a man in a red sweater strikes him with a club each time he charges. Buck learns that he cannot win that fight by strength alone.

Buck travels north with other dogs. The air grows colder, and the ground is covered with snow. He watches a dog named Curly greet another animal, only to be attacked and killed by a group. The sudden violence teaches Buck to keep his distance when dogs are fighting. He has never had to search for a safe place to sleep or defend his food before. Now he watches the older dogs closely and learns where to curl beneath the snow for warmth.

Men put Buck into a harness with a sled team. He does not know the commands, so the dogs and drivers show him when to pull, stop, and turn. The work is hard, and a mistake can slow the whole team. Buck watches the leader Spitz, who is quick and experienced but ready to threaten a weaker dog. Each day Buck understands a little more of the new rules. He has not forgotten his old home, yet he must learn the northern trail if he is to survive there.`,
    b2More:[
      'Buck had been treated almost like a member of the household. The sale changes his life in a single afternoon, but the journey north takes many stages. At each one he waits for someone he knows to appear.',
      'The man with the club does not need to chase Buck; he waits until the dog attacks and then strikes. Buck learns to stand back, though he does not come to like the man. Curly’s death shows him another danger that no human will explain.',
      'The leather traces connect Buck’s effort to the heavy sled. He feels the pull when the team starts and the strain when one dog falls behind. Watching Spitz teaches him as much as the drivers’ shouted commands.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-call-of-wild-2': {
    sharedMore:'At one camp Buck digs a hole in the snow to sleep. Spitz takes the place before Buck can settle in it. Buck bares his teeth, and the dogs circle each other until another sound draws them away. The quarrel stays with them when they are harnessed for the next day’s run.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Buck pulls a sled across the northern trail with Spitz and the other dogs. The team travels long distances in cold weather and stops wherever the drivers can make camp. Buck learns to curl under the snow for warmth and to take food before another dog can steal it. He listens for commands and watches how each dog keeps the sled moving. Spitz leads the team, but he uses his experience to threaten Buck. The two dogs challenge each other more often as Buck becomes stronger.

One night a rabbit runs from the camp. Buck and Spitz chase it across the snow. Buck nearly reaches the animal, but Spitz cuts across his path. The two dogs turn toward each other and fight away from the team. Spitz is used to winning, yet Buck changes the way he attacks. He catches Spitz off balance and brings him down. The other dogs close in on the fallen leader. Buck returns to the sled knowing that the place at its front is now open.

The next morning the drivers try to put another dog in the lead position. Buck refuses to move back. He avoids their hands and stands where Spitz stood. After several attempts they let him take the place. Buck responds quickly when the path bends and keeps the other dogs pulling together. The team makes better time under him. He has won a fight, but he must also show that he can guide the team through each day's work.`,
    b2More:[
      'The dogs’ traces pull tight when the sled starts, and every delay is felt by the others. Buck learns to read the ground ahead and to move before the drivers have to shout. His skill makes Spitz watch him more closely.',
      'The chase draws them away from the camp. Buck cannot beat Spitz by rushing straight at him, so he changes direction and catches his rival’s legs. When the fight ends, the pack moves toward the fallen leader.',
      'Buck circles away whenever the drivers try to place him behind another dog. He returns to the front until they give up. On the trail his quick turns and steady pace show that he wanted more than a prize from the fight.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-call-of-wild-3': {
    sharedMore:'The new owners pack too much onto the sled and do not carry enough food. They argue about what to leave behind while the dogs stand waiting in the cold. As the journey continues, several animals die and the others weaken. Buck has pulled through hard weather before, but this time his body cannot recover between the long days.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`After Buck becomes leader, the sled team changes hands. Its new owners do not understand the northern trail. They load the sled with too many belongings and fail to plan enough food for the dogs. The team grows weaker each day. Several dogs die, and Buck becomes too thin to pull well. At a river crossing in spring he falls in the traces and cannot rise. The owners shout and prepare to beat him forward, though the ice ahead has begun to break.

John Thornton sees the exhausted dog and steps between Buck and the people holding the whip. He warns them that the ice will not bear the sled. They refuse to listen. Thornton cuts Buck out of the harness and pulls him away. The rest of the team moves onto the river with its owners. The ice gives way, and they disappear beneath the water. Buck survives because Thornton would not let him take another step.

Thornton feeds Buck and gives him time to recover. Buck follows the man around the camp, sometimes taking his hand gently in his mouth. He has known harsh drivers, but this one treats him with care. As his strength returns, Buck explores the trees beyond camp. He hears distant calls from the forest and stays away a little longer each time. Then he comes back to Thornton. The man who saved him is still waiting there, and Buck has a place beside him.`,
    b2More:[
      'The owners believe that more shouting will make up for poor planning. Empty food bags and a heavy sled show otherwise. Buck’s legs fail before they reach the river, but they try to force him onward.',
      'Thornton has watched the spring ice and understands how quickly it is breaking. He cuts the straps while the owners protest. The empty place where Buck stood is all that remains of the team after the crossing.',
      'Recovery is slow. Buck sleeps and eats before he can roam again. He returns from his first longer walks in the forest to find Thornton at the camp, and that welcome draws him back again.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-around-world-1': {
    sharedMore:'Passepartout had hoped that his new job would be quiet. At the station he finds Fogg ready to leave with a small bag and a large sum of money. They board a train before the servant has time to settle into the household routine. Fogg checks the schedule while Passepartout wonders what the next stop will demand.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Phileas Fogg follows an exact daily routine in London. At the Reform Club he reads new travel times for railways and steamships and says a person can circle the world in eighty days. Other members doubt him, so he bets a large sum that he can do it. That evening he leaves with his new servant Passepartout. They travel by train and ship toward Suez, then continue for India. Fogg counts the days carefully and plans each connection.

Detective Fix watches Fogg at Suez. A bank has been robbed in London, and Fix mistakenly believes the traveller is the thief. He wants an arrest warrant to arrive, but Fogg keeps moving from port to port. Fix follows without telling Fogg why. In India the travellers board a train, expecting the line to carry them across the country. Part of the railway is unfinished. Fogg buys an elephant and hires a guide to take them through the forest to the next station.

During that journey, the party learns that a young woman called Aouda is in danger. Fogg chooses to stop and rescue her, though the delay may cost him the wager. Passepartout helps in the rescue, and they leave with Aouda on the elephant. She has no safe way to remain behind, so the group takes her onward. They reach the railway and continue the journey. Fogg still watches the timetable, but he has spent some of its precious hours helping someone he did not know when he left London.`,
    b2More:[
      'Fogg’s sudden departure surprises Passepartout, who thought his new employer would never change a routine. The servant has little time to pack before the wager begins.',
      'Fix sends word ahead about his suspicion but cannot stop the traveller at Suez. He listens to Passepartout speak freely about the route and uses each departure to follow them.',
      'The elephant carries them over ground the train cannot cross. The guide leads it through forest paths that the travellers could never have found alone. Aouda’s rescue demands a quick decision, and Fogg chooses her safety even while calculating the hours he will lose.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-around-world-3': {
    sharedMore:'The train crosses bridges and open country while Fogg checks the timetable. Aouda and Passepartout watch the landscape change from their carriage. Fix stays close to the group; although he still suspects Fogg, he now wants him to reach England, where an arrest can be made. The journey cannot wait for every obstacle to clear. At each stop they look for the next departure and listen for news about the line ahead.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Fogg, Aouda, and Passepartout arrive in San Francisco and board a train east across the United States. Detective Fix continues to follow them. He still suspects Fogg of stealing from the bank, but he needs the party to reach England before an arrest can happen. The train crosses wide plains and difficult tracks. Fogg watches the schedule while the others look for the next delay that might cost him the wager.

During the crossing, attackers board the train. Passepartout is carried away in the confusion. The train moves on, but Fogg refuses to leave his servant behind. He takes a small group of men back across the plain to search. They find Passepartout alive and bring him to Aouda. The rescue has cost time, and the original train is gone. Fogg must find another way to make the next connection. Aouda sees how much he values the people travelling with him, even though he rarely speaks about his feelings.

The party hires a wind-powered sled to cross snowy ground and catch up with the route. It moves quickly, but the weather and the distance make the ride uncertain. They reach the coast and prepare for the Atlantic crossing. Passepartout is safe, and the group remains together after the attack. Fogg has given up hours to save him. The wager is still possible, but every lost connection leaves less time to reach the Reform Club in London.`,
    b2More:[
      'The train’s route takes the party through places none of them knows well. Fix watches for a chance to keep Fogg moving toward Britain, where his warrant can finally be used.',
      'Fogg goes back with armed companions while the train continues without him. Passepartout cannot know whether anyone will come. Their reunion leaves no time for a long celebration; they must move again.',
      'The sled uses wind in place of an engine. The travellers lean into the cold as it runs across the plain. A ship across the Atlantic is still needed before the final race to London can begin.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-around-world-4': {
    sharedMore:'At the harbour Fogg learns that the usual steamer has already gone. He searches for another captain willing to sail and boards the Henrietta. Its captain has a different destination, so Fogg offers the crew money to change course. Passepartout watches the argument and hopes that the strange bargain will still bring them home in time. There is no other ship ready to help them.',
    sharedIndex:0,
    b1:`Fogg and his companions need a ship across the Atlantic to finish their journey. No captain will take them on exactly the route they need, so Fogg pays for a vessel and changes its course. Fuel runs low; the crew burns wooden parts of the ship to keep it moving. They reach the British coast and take a train toward London. Fogg believes he has only a small amount of time left before the eighty-day deadline.

At Liverpool, Fix arrests him for the bank robbery. Fogg cannot continue while the detective holds him. Then news arrives that the real robber has been caught elsewhere. Fix releases him, but the next train has left. Fogg finally reaches London and returns home believing he has missed the club's deadline. Aouda asks him to marry her. He agrees, although he thinks the wager and much of his money are lost. Passepartout goes to arrange their wedding.

While making the arrangements, Passepartout learns that the day is Saturday, not Sunday as they believed. The party travelled east around the world and crossed time zones in a way that gave them an extra calendar day. He races home with the news. Fogg hurries to the Reform Club and arrives before the deadline. He wins the wager after all. Aouda and Passepartout have shared the delays and dangers that brought him there, and they are with him when the long journey ends.`,
    b2More:[
      'The captain objects to changing course, but Fogg uses his money to keep the ship heading toward Britain. Passepartout watches pieces of the vessel disappear into the furnace as the engine needs more fuel.',
      'Fix’s arrest comes almost at the end of the route he has followed for weeks. Even after the mistake is revealed, Fogg cannot bring back the train that left while he was detained. He goes home in silence.',
      'Passepartout sees the true date when he asks about the wedding. He rushes back before Fogg can settle into defeat. The final run to the club is possible because they recognize the day gained by travelling east.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-secret-garden-1': {
    sharedMore:'Martha tells Mary about a skipping rope and shows her how to use it. Mary is clumsy at first and has to stop for breath, but she tries again outside. The exercise keeps her in the garden longer. Ben Weatherstaff continues his work nearby, and the robin hops close enough for her to watch without speaking.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`Mary Lennox grows up in India while servants do nearly everything for her. A sickness kills her parents and many people in their household. She is sent to England to live with her uncle at Misselthwaite Manor. Mrs. Medlock brings her through its long corridors and tells her that her uncle stays away and that many rooms are closed. Mary is used to being obeyed, but the people in this house have work of their own. She spends lonely days inside until a servant called Martha speaks to her about the moor outside.

Martha talks plainly to Mary and tells her about her family. Mary starts to walk in the fresh air. She meets Ben Weatherstaff, a gardener who does not hurry to please her, and notices a robin near a walled garden. She hears that the garden has been locked since her aunt died. No one will show her its door. Mary returns to the wall and watches where the bird lands, looking for a way to learn more about the hidden place.

One day the robin hops beside a patch of earth. Mary sees something buried there and digs out a small key. She remembers Martha's story about the locked garden. She has not found its door yet, so she keeps the key and searches along the wall. The quiet house still holds many closed rooms, but outside Mary now has a question to follow. She goes back to look for the robin and the place it seems to know.`,
    b2More:[
      'Mary had rarely needed to do anything for herself before the sickness. At the manor she waits for someone to attend to her, but the long corridors remain empty. Martha’s direct talk surprises her.',
      'Ben Weatherstaff works while Mary asks questions. He says little about the garden, yet the robin moves near the same wall each day. Mary begins to return without anyone telling her to.',
      'The key is small and rusty from the earth. Mary turns it in her hand and looks at the ivy-covered wall. She cannot ask her uncle where the door is because he is away, so she must keep searching herself.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-secret-garden-2': {
    sharedMore:'Mary brings tools into the garden and learns to clear dead stems without hurting the green shoots beneath them. Dickon arrives with seeds and a spade. He understands which roots may grow again. The children keep their work quiet because Mary fears that an adult might lock the door once more. They make plans for tomorrow.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`Mary follows the robin along the garden wall and finds a hidden door. The key she dug from the earth turns in its lock. Inside, branches cover the paths and dead stems tangle around the beds. Mary looks closely and sees green shoots growing beneath the dry plants. She begins to clear a small space and returns whenever she can. She keeps the discovery secret because she does not want the door closed again.

Martha's brother Dickon knows plants and animals. Mary trusts him with the secret and shows him the garden. He brings tools and seeds, and together they decide what can grow. Dickon helps her move dead growth without harming the living roots. Mary works outdoors for longer each day. She begins to look forward to seeing what has changed since the last visit. The garden feels less abandoned as they make clear places for new plants.

At night Mary hears a child crying somewhere in the manor. She follows the sound through the corridors and finds Colin, her uncle's son, in a closed room. He has been kept apart from other children and believes he will never walk. He expects people to obey him, and Mary argues with him instead. She tells him about the world outside his window, though she does not yet reveal every part of her secret. Colin asks her to come back. Mary has found another person in the house who wants to know what lies beyond its closed doors.`,
    b2More:[
      'The buried key works, though the door is nearly hidden by plants. Mary steps inside and sees that the garden is not dead. She studies each green point before pulling anything from the ground.',
      'Dickon treats the garden as a place that needs patient work. He brings seeds and shows Mary how to look for new growth. They kneel among the beds and make plans for their next visit.',
      'Colin’s room is far from the garden, but he has heard many stories about his health. Mary does not speak to him as if every word might hurt him. Their quarrel gives him a reason to ask her back.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-secret-garden-3': {
    sharedMore:'Colin first sees the garden from his chair. Dickon shows him birds and new leaves while Mary points out the beds they have cleared. On later visits Colin asks to come closer to the plants. He wants to touch them and help, even when a small task takes time. Mary and Dickon make room for him beside them.',
    sharedIndex:1,
    b1:`Mary tells Colin about the hidden garden and asks whether he wants to see it. He has spent so long inside that the idea frightens him, yet he wants to know why Mary and Dickon return there every day. They arrange for him to be taken out in his chair without drawing attention from adults who might stop the visit. He sees the paths, birds, and green shoots for himself. The place is different from the sickroom where he has spent most of his time.

Dickon points out young plants and the work the children have done. Colin begins by watching from his chair. Soon he asks for a closer look and wants a task of his own. Mary and Dickon help him take part. They return together, and Colin starts to move more. He tries standing with support and is surprised by what he can do. He asks his friends to keep his progress private until he is ready to tell his father.

Mary has changed since she arrived at the manor too. She no longer waits alone for servants to entertain her. Each morning she goes outside to meet Dickon and Colin. The three look for new shoots, work in the soil, and talk about their next visit. Colin still has difficult days, but he now has people beside him and a place he wants to reach again. He begins to imagine meeting his father outdoors rather than from his bed.`,
    b2More:[
      'Colin once expected every pain to mean disaster. In the garden he hears Dickon talk about seedlings and listens to birds instead of adults whispering about his condition.',
      'Standing is not easy at first. Colin tries while Mary and Dickon stay close, then sits down to rest. They return another day rather than asking him to do everything at once.',
      'The children measure their time by the changes in the beds they tend. Mary notices that she now looks for her friends when she wakes. Colin talks about showing his father the path he has walked and the plants growing beside it.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-frankenstein-1': {
    sharedMore: 'At Ingolstadt, Victor first meets teachers who challenge his old reading. He attends lectures and learns new ways to study chemistry. Their work makes him think he can reach a secret that others have missed. The longer he works, the less he writes to Elizabeth and his father. He keeps his door shut while his friends wonder why he has disappeared from their lives.',
    b1: `Robert Walton writes to his sister from a ship sailing into Arctic ice. He hopes to find a northern passage and make a great discovery. One day the crew sees a huge figure travelling over the frozen sea. The next day they rescue a weaker traveller whose sled has broken down. The man is Victor Frankenstein. He asks where the ship is going, and Walton tells him about his plans. Victor hears the explorer's hunger for discovery and says that his own story may serve as a warning.

Victor begins with his childhood in Geneva. Elizabeth grows up in his family, and Henry Clerval is his close friend. At university Victor studies the natural world and becomes determined to discover how life begins. He works alone for months, bringing materials to his room and ignoring letters from home. He forgets the seasons outside while he builds a body. Victor imagines that the new being will thank him, but he has not prepared for the moment when it opens its eyes.

On a rainy night his experiment succeeds. The creature moves, and its appearance terrifies Victor. He leaves the room and tries to sleep, but wakes from a frightening dream to find the creature standing beside his bed. It reaches toward him. Victor runs away again and wanders through the streets until morning. There he meets Henry, who has just arrived to study. Henry expects to find his friend at work, but Victor can scarcely speak about the laboratory. Henry looks after him through a long illness. When Victor eventually returns to his room, the creature is gone. He has no idea where it went or what will happen to it.`,
    b2More: [
      'Walton has wanted a friend who understands his ambition. Victor listens closely before deciding to tell him what happened in Geneva. The ship is trapped among ice when this long account begins.',
      'Victor visits places connected with death to learn how bodies are made. His work keeps him away from the people who care for him. He believes success is near and refuses to pause for rest.',
      'Henry sees Victor shaking and feverish but cannot learn why. Victor cannot bring himself to describe the being he left behind. He lets Henry care for him while keeping the secret that caused his collapse.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-around-world-2': {
    sharedMore: 'Passepartout had hoped to protect his master by watching Fix, but the detective uses the servant’s own delay against him. On board, Passepartout looks for Fogg and realizes he has sailed without him. When he reaches Japan, he cannot pay for a room. The performance troupe gives him a temporary place to sleep and a way to continue searching.',
    b1: `The travellers reach Calcutta after their journey across India. Passepartout is charged because he entered a temple with his shoes on during an earlier stop. Detective Fix has helped bring the complaint; he wants Fogg delayed until an arrest warrant arrives. Fogg pays the fine so the group can catch its ship. Aouda travels with them to Hong Kong, where they hope to find one of her relatives. The relative has moved away. Fogg invites Aouda to continue with him rather than leave her alone in an unfamiliar port.

Fix still wants to keep Fogg in British territory. He tells Passepartout that he suspects Fogg of stealing from a bank, but the servant does not tell his master. When Fix learns that their next ship will leave earlier than expected, he gives Passepartout drink and keeps him from carrying the news. Passepartout reaches the vessel, but Fogg and Aouda miss it. They go to the quay and find that their servant has vanished with the ship. Fogg hires a smaller boat in rough weather, hoping to catch another connection. He cannot know where Passepartout will land.

Passepartout reaches Yokohama with little money. He looks for food and work while searching for his companions. A performance troupe offers him a place, and he joins its show. Fogg and Aouda arrive by another route and find him there. The three are relieved to be together again, although the separation has cost them time. They prepare to cross the Pacific, still followed by Fix and still facing the deadline of Fogg's wager.`,
    b2More: [
      'At the court Fogg wastes no time arguing once the penalty is set. The next departure matters more to him than winning the dispute. Passepartout knows his mistake has become expensive.',
      'The little boat is tossed by the sea. Fogg watches for a ship that can carry them toward Japan while Aouda waits with him. They have no way to contact the servant who sailed ahead.',
      'Passepartout earns a place in the show because he can perform in its human pyramid. When Fogg spots him, the act breaks apart in surprise. Their reunion brings them back onto the same route.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-secret-garden-4': {
    sharedIndex: 0,
    sharedMore: 'The garden is no longer covered in dead branches. Mary and Dickon have cleared paths, planted seeds, and watched new leaves appear. Colin knows that his father remembers the place from before it was locked. He wants their first meeting to happen among the living plants. Every day he asks his friends for another chance to practise walking down the path.',
    b1: `Colin returns to the hidden garden with Mary and Dickon whenever he can. At first he needed his chair to reach the beds. Now he practises standing and walking among the plants. The children keep his progress quiet because he wants his father to see it for himself. They work in the soil and watch the garden grow while Colin tries to go a little farther each day. Mary and Dickon are beside him when he needs to rest. He talks about meeting his father outside instead of being visited in his sickroom.

Archibald Craven has stayed away from Misselthwaite Manor since his wife died. He travels with memories of the garden she loved but cannot bring himself to visit it. A dream about her and a letter from his brother draw him home. He walks through the grounds toward the long-closed garden. From beyond the wall he hears children's voices. He stops, surprised that anyone has entered a place locked for years, then follows the sound toward the hidden door.

Inside, Colin has been practising with his friends. He is standing when his father arrives. Archibald can hardly believe that the boy before him is his son. Colin moves toward him, and they embrace among the plants. Mary and Dickon watch their meeting. Father and son leave the garden together and walk back toward the house. The servants see them coming side by side. Mary, who once waited alone in the silent manor, has helped open both the garden and the family's life again.`,
    b2More: [
      'Colin remembers how frightened he once was of leaving his room. Each new visit has made the garden more familiar. He wants his father to see the work and the steps for himself.',
      'Craven expects the same closed house he left. He does not know Mary found the key or that the children have cared for the garden. Their voices make him hurry to the door.',
      'For years the garden had held painful memories for him. Now he finds his son there with friends. Colin walks beside him on the way back, and the household sees the change without needing a long explanation.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-call-of-wild-4': {
    sharedIndex: 0,
    sharedMore: 'Buck had once slept beside a human fire every night. Now he can spend days alone, finding his way by smell and sound. The wolf he follows waits for him in the trees, then races ahead to show him a trail. Buck turns back to Thornton several times, even when the forest offers food. That choice ends only when the camp is destroyed. After the attack, he pauses by the empty fire and recognizes that Thornton will not call him back again.',
    b1: `Buck leaves Thornton's camp for longer journeys into the forest. He hunts and meets a wild wolf. At first the wolf keeps its distance, but they begin to run together. Buck follows it for a time, then returns to the camp where Thornton welcomes him. The calls beyond the trees continue to draw him away. He learns the paths, finds food, and comes back from each journey to the man who saved him.

One day Buck returns and smells danger before he reaches the tents. The camp has been attacked. He finds Thornton and his companions dead. Men from a nearby group are still there, and Buck charges at them. They scatter, but Buck cannot bring Thornton back. He searches the place where his friend lived and waits for a voice that no longer comes. The fire and tents cannot give him a reason to stay. He goes into the trees and meets the wolf again.

Other wolves approach Buck and test him. He fights when they challenge him, then runs with their pack. His strength and knowledge of the forest help him lead them. The people in the region later speak of a large dog moving among wolves. In summer Buck returns to the place where Thornton died. He stands there for a while, then leaves the old camp and follows the pack into the forest.`,
    b2More: [
      'The wolf is wary of Buck at first. They sniff the air around each other and then run along the same trail. Buck chooses to turn back when he thinks of Thornton waiting at camp.',
      'Buck follows the attackers through the camp before driving them away. Their flight leaves a terrible quiet behind. He noses around the place where Thornton once sat, but no one answers him.',
      'The first wolves do not welcome a stranger at once. Buck has to hold his ground and learn their movements. Later he can run at their head, but the old campsite remains a place he visits alone.',
    ],
  },
  'book6-treasure-island-3': {
    sharedMore: 'Ben Gunn says he has been alone for three years. He talks of cheese, food he has missed since the other pirates abandoned him. Jim promises to tell the doctor where to find him. When Jim reaches the stockade, the loyal group has already lost men bringing supplies ashore. They have to guard the fence, tend the wounded, and keep watch for Silver’s next move.',
    b1: `The Hispaniola reaches the island, and Jim goes ashore with some of Silver's men. He slips away among the trees and hears Silver argue with a sailor who refuses to join the mutiny. Silver kills the man. Jim runs through the undergrowth with no weapon and no safe path back to the landing place. A wild-looking stranger steps out before him: Ben Gunn, a former pirate left on the island years earlier. Ben asks whether Dr. Livesey is with Jim and begs for a passage home. He hints that the treasure hunt may surprise the men who have Flint's map.

Meanwhile the doctor, Squire Trelawney, Captain Smollett, and their loyal companions have left the ship for an old stockade. They make several dangerous trips in a small boat, carrying powder, food, and other supplies while pirates watch from shore. The fence gives them a place to defend, and a spring supplies water. Jim rejoins them there and tells the doctor about Ben Gunn. Outside the walls, Silver gathers the mutineers and prepares to demand the map.

Silver approaches under a flag and offers safety if Smollett surrenders Flint's map. The captain refuses. Silver leaves angrily, and the defenders take positions around the fence. Pirates rush the stockade. Smoke makes it hard to see who is moving between the trees, and shots come from several directions. The loyal group drives the attackers back, but some of their own people are killed or wounded. Jim stays inside with the others, knowing that the pirates still hold much of the island.`,
    b2More: [
      'Jim hears the loyal sailor refuse Silver and then hears the cry that follows. He has seen enough to know that Silver will kill anyone who stands in the mutineers’ way. Ben Gunn speaks quickly after years with almost no company.',
      'The doctor’s party has to move its supplies before the pirates control the shore. The small boat sits low in the water as they carry the last load. They choose the stockade because its fence and spring offer a chance to hold out.',
      'The men inside the fence wait until the attackers come close. After the fight they count the people who can still defend the walls and care for the wounded. Their victory gives them shelter, but it does not give them the ship.',
    ],
  },
};
